@file PFR/Kullback.lean
@decl KLDiv
-- L4DEL: title: If `X, Y` are two `G`-valued random variables, the Kullback--Leibler divergence is defined as `KL(X ‖ Y) := ∑ₓ 𝐏(X = x) log (𝐏(X = x) / 𝐏(Y = x))`
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `X, Y` are two `G`-valued random variables, the Kullback--Leibler divergence is defined as `KL(X ‖ Y) := ∑ₓ 𝐏(X = x) log (𝐏(X = x) / 𝐏(Y = x))`. Note that this definition only makes sense when `X` is absolutely continuous wrt to `Y`, i.e., `∀ x, 𝐏(Y = x) = 0 → 𝐏(X = x) = 0`. Otherwise, the divergence should be infinite, but since we use real numbers for ease of computations, this is not a possible choice.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

